  "interpretation": [
    "Across all 80 measured Glass traces, the nil event is an effective-appearance callback and falls in panel.attach_content_to_foreground for both variants; no surface.move.nil event is observed.",
    "In original, attach_content_to_foreground is the first attachment checkpoint and also includes preceding background/foreground setup. In foreground_first, the empty foreground has already joined the window and its checkpoint has returned; the nil effective-appearance callback occurs while adding content to that already-windowed foreground, before the content.window pointer becomes non-nil.",
    "No nil effective-appearance event occurs in open initial placement, prime, or final placement. foreground_first evidence excludes the earlier shared frame/autoresizing setup as the trigger for its nil event.",
    "Both variants retain three content refreshes per measured open: one nil Aqua effective-appearance refresh, one attached Aqua move refresh, then one attached DarkAqua effective-appearance refresh during the late appearance setter.",
    "This leaves a conditional interaction mechanism: early dark window appearance plus foreground-first could let the pre-window-pointer content appearance callback already resolve DarkAqua, potentially avoiding the later attached color transition. Neither one-factor experiment demonstrated this, so callback counts and full call time must be measured before treating the combination as an optimization.",
    "Callback durations include nested refreshAppearance durations; do not sum these with the containing panel/open stages or each other."
  ]
}
